        "number": "3.4",
        "title": "The Cousins We Left Behind",
        "blocks": [
          "While one branch of African primates wandered out across the world, several other branches stayed in the equatorial rainforest of the Congo basin and developed lives entirely unlike our own.",
          "Three closely related apes now share that vast green canopy.",
          "Chimpanzees, our nearest cousin by genetic measure, live in restless political troops in which an alpha male prevails through carefully maintained alliances and occasional outbursts of strategic violence.",
          "Bonobos, who differ from chimpanzees only slightly in anatomy, have devised an utterly different solution to the problem of conflict: in bonobo society, almost every potential dispute is mediated through sexual contact, and the dominant individuals are typically female.",
          "Gorillas, the largest of the three and gentle vegetarians, live in quiet mountain harems guided by a single silverback whose authority is rarely challenged.",
          "Three apes, each genetically almost identical to the others, yet each demonstrates a radically distinct answer to the same evolutionary problem of how to organise a society."
        ],
