          "Even the cities of the continent that no human ever designed prove cognitively impressive: army ants link themselves into a temporary fortress called a bivouac each evening and march to new ground at dawn, while termite mounds ascend several metres above the savannah, their intricate ventilation shafts coordinating an internal climate that fluctuates by no more than a degree across a thirty-degree day."
