        "blocks": [
          "European linguistics across the twentieth century articulated the discipline that studies how human language actually operates.",
          "The Swiss linguist Ferdinand de Saussure articulated, in lectures published only after his death, the structural distinction between the abstract system of language and the individual moment of speech, opening the entire structuralist tradition.",
          "The American Noam Chomsky proposed, with worldwide consequences, that every human infant arrives with a universal grammar already encoded in the brain, an inherited syntax template that any specific language fills in with its local vocabulary.",
          "Modern linguistics divides into phonetics, the study of physical sound; syntax, the study of grammatical structure; semantics, the study of meaning; pragmatics, the study of context; and discourse analysis, the study of larger conversational structure.",
          "The lexicon of each language remains the most resistant element to translation; bilingual children navigate two lexicons simultaneously from infancy, switching effortlessly as the social context shifts.",
          "Translation theorists articulated the impossibility of perfect equivalence between languages, the unavoidable cohesion of metaphor with culture, the nuance lost in every direct translation, and the temporal flavour of every word as it shifts across generations, alongside the role of intonation, anecdote, eloquence, innuendo, and other nonverbal cues in shaping the rhetoric that listeners actually receive.",
          "The beneficial cognitive effect of growing up bilingual has been repeatedly demonstrated in longitudinal studies.",
          "The relation between language and thought, however, remains a tenuous theoretical area: the strong Sapir-Whorf hypothesis has been treated as a fallacy by some and as a partial truth by others, while scepticism about translation equivalence has only deepened.",
          "European universities accordingly restructured their language curriculum to incorporate translation as a discipline of its own, with a dedicated syllabus and a recognised pedagogy."
        ],
